questionFive     = "Who Receives Zakat?";

answerOne        = "Zakat is one of the five pillars of Islam and refers to the obligation that an individual has to donate a certain proportion of wealth each year to charitable causes. It is an act of worship and purification that helps those in need.";
answerTwo        = "Any Muslim who possesses wealth above the Nisab threshold for one lunar year must pay Zakat. The person must have full ownership of the wealth and it must be in excess of their basic needs.";
answerThree      = "Nisab is the minimum amount of wealth a Muslim must possess before they become obligated to pay Zakat. It is equivalent to 87.48 grams of gold or 612.36 grams of silver, whichever is lower in value.";
answerFour       = "Zakat is calculated at 2.5% of your total qualifying wealth that has been in your possession for one lunar year. This includes cash, savings, gold, silver, business assets, and investments.";
answerFive       = "The Quran identifies eight categories of people who are eligible to receive Zakat: the poor, the needy, those employed to collect Zakat, those whose hearts are to be reconciled, captives, debtors, in the cause of Allah, and travelers in need.";
